{
  "id": "COMPOUND:40489 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a relationship between 1,7-Dihydroxy-2-methylanthracene-9,10-dione and DNA polymerase iota, as this compound is identified as a target of multiple entities that are themselves targets of DNA polymerase iota. Specifically, the compound is linked to FaDu, a cell line that is targeted by several structurally diverse molecules, including 1H-Inden-1-one, 4-(beta-D-glucopyranosyloxy)-2,3-dihydro-6-(2-hydroxyethyl)-2,2,5,7-tetramethyl-, and 16-Heptadecene-1,2,4-triol, all of which are known to interact with DNA polymerase iota. This indicates that 1,7-Dihydroxy-2-methylanthracene-9,10-dione may serve as a common intermediary or ligand in a pathway that ultimately leads to DNA polymerase iota activation. The consistent presence of this compound in the target list of DNA polymerase iota suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the compound's interaction with the enzyme, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be elucidated.",
